  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "FY2026 lands inside the reaffirmed $7.70-8.10 adjusted-EPS range on ~5.5% reported sales growth (H1 +6.9%, FX-assisted). From FY2027 PPG grows at its end markets plus a little: aerospace coatings (backlog ~$315M, double-digit organic) and packaging (BPA-NI reformulation) offset flat European auto OEM and architectural, giving ~3-3.5% nominal growth. The ~$175M cost program and the elimination of stranded costs from the US/Canada architectural and silicas exits lift pre-SBC EBITDA margin from ~17.3% to 18.5% by FY2029, roughly half the gross savings retained after price give-backs on index-based industrial contracts. Capex tapers from the FY2024-25 capacity step-up (4.5-4.9% of sales) back toward ~3.2%, which is where most of the cash-flow improvement comes from.",
    "bull": "The portfolio simplification works as management pitches it: the remaining business is a specialty-coatings mix (aerospace, refinish, packaging, protective) that grows mid-single-digit, Europe recovers from its 2023-26 trough, LatAm architectural keeps growing, and the full cost savings drop through, taking margin to 20% (still below Sherwin-Williams). Buybacks continue at the FY2025 pace. Exit at 17x owner FCF, in line with where coatings peers trade in normal markets.",
    "bear": "Industrial Coatings takes the hit the analysis flags: an auto-OEM and European industrial downturn in FY2027 flattens sales, and falling raw-material indices reset contract prices downward, so margin drops ~110bp and the cost program only offsets price erosion. PPG, now without the US/Canada architectural repaint base that cushioned past cycles, recovers only to ~16% margin and 2% growth. Not a solvency case: interest cover ~8x and net debt ~2x EBITDA. Exit at 12x."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "PPG is valued as a mature, moderately cyclical specialty-coatings franchise on a strict owner-FCF basis, unlevered, at an 8.5% USD WACC with a 12-17x terminal cap. Value hinges on two levers: whether the cost program lifts margins (~120bp in base) and whether capex normalizes from the FY2024-25 step-up. Divestiture losses and the Russia impairment are non-cash/discontinued and do not enter the flows. SBC is small (~0.4% of revenue incl. withholding), so house and street bases sit close together.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Auto OEM and European industrial cyclicality hitting Industrial Coatings volumes and index-linked pricing",
      "Cost-program savings competed away rather than retained in margin",
      "Capex staying near 4.5% of revenue instead of tapering to ~3.2%",
      "FX: a large share of revenue is outside the US, and FX helped FY2025/H1 2026",
      "Terminal cap multiple: the base binds at 15x; each 1x is roughly $5/share"
    ]
  },
